\title{navier-stokes-euler is the Mathlib mechanism in a larger dose; cslib is a smaller dose; con-leche is flat}
\taxon{Insight}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{performance}
\meta{origin}{docs/research/profiles/P9.md}
\meta{updated}{2026-11-24}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Source: [report P9](rep-P9); [the profiling session](ses-JRSD). One worker, native image \code{\verb~|-O3~}, the default switch set of the machine as published at 21:02Z on 2026-11-24, instructions and thread CPU inside the checks per unit; the leaders per declaration with their arena builds in the same hour; CPU shares from one quiet window.}
\table{
\tr{\th{Corpus}\th{Units}\th{Machine / gruszka, instructions}\th{Removed by the two shortcuts}\th{Eager-reduction declines, share of the worker}\th{The rest}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler}\td{124,850}\td{7.98}\td{54.5 percent of the instructions, 51.2 percent of the CPU}\td{1.5 percent}\td{3.63 times gruszka}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{397,608}\td{4.12}\td{22.4 percent, 14.6 percent of the CPU}\td{12.6 percent}\td{3.20 times}}
\tr{\td{con-leche}\td{36,274}\td{3.24}\td{none: +9.2 percent instructions, +5.6 percent CPU}\td{2.9 percent}\td{3.24 times, flat}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib, from P8}\td{714,712}\td{6.52}\td{40.5 percent}\td{11 to 12 percent}\td{3.88 times}}
}
\ul{
\li{On navier-stokes-euler 84 percent of the instructions are in the 57,330 units of the project itself, at 9.35 times gruszka. The proofs compared are those of analysis: the two instance paths of the reals, bundled maps, and subobjects.}
\li{A second class is not touched by \code{\verb~|skipargs~} and repaired only by \code{\verb~|congruence~}, 14 percent of the corpus: applications of one head, or one term under two environments, unfolded on both sides instead of compared by their arguments.}
\li{Nothing about numerals: literal arithmetic is 70,600 folding events and recursor reduction 277,300 events of 553 M.}
\li{cslib is 79 percent Mathlib and its dependencies by cost. On con-leche every kind of unit with more than 1 percent of the cost is between 2.6 and 3.9 times; the machine makes 1.30 times the steps of gruszka and 0.62 times its values, and the factor is instructions per step, 1,979 against 793.}
\li{The two options are not a free win: on con-leche \code{\verb~|congruence~} costs 8.4 percent of the instructions, because in one unit the congruence walks cost more than the unfoldings they replace. Since 2026-11-24 the congruence segments of one declaration have [a cap](log-9636), and with it con-leche is no slower with the shortcuts than without.}
\li{In CPU time the ratios are smaller than in instructions, about 3.2, 1.6 and 1.2 for the checks alone, because the machine retires 15 to 18 G instructions per second and gruszka 6 to 7.}
}
\transclude{ex-VC9X}
